# GPU experiments go through the queue

This machine's GPUs are shared by several agents. Never launch training,
fine-tuning, or any CUDA workload directly. Always use `exp`:

    exp submit --label <short-name> -- <command>      # returns a task id
    exp wait <id>                                     # blocks; exit code is the job's
    exp log <id> [-n 50]                              # output so far
    exp status --active                               # what is queued/running
    exp gpus                                          # per-GPU view
    exp kill <id>                                     # abandon a run

Rules:
- One run per GPU. `exp submit` picks the least-loaded card and sets
  CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES for you. Do not set it yourself.
- Write all outputs (checkpoints, metrics, plots) under `$EXP_RUN_DIR`.
  It is a fresh directory per run; `meta.json` and `log.txt` land there.
- Quick CPU-only checks (unit tests, data prep, evals under a minute) can run
  directly. Anything longer goes through `exp submit --gpu none`.
- Do not `exp kill` a task you did not submit.
- Work in your own git worktree so file edits do not collide with other agents.
